 perhaps; modello unc/uncertain; ipotesi scartata nel testo","significato":"ball","termine_origine":"","catena":[{"codice":"la-med","nome":"Medieval Latin","termine":"","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"la-med","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Uncertain. Perhaps from the variant boașă, possibly from Medieval Latin, Late Latin bursa (“purse”), although this is unlikely. Perhaps from Turkish boş, although this is also doubtful due to the age of the word. Compare Aromanian boashã, Megleno-Romanian and Istro-Romanian boş."},{"parola":"băiat","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"non_precisato","incerta":"sì","motivo_incerta":"Possibly","significato":"boy","termine_origine":"balietus","catena":[{"codice":"la-med","nome":"Medieval Latin","termine":"balietus","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"la-med","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Most likely originated as the past participle of băia (“to care for an infant”), in which case possibly influenced by bărbat in derivation. Compare băiată (“girl”). Other theories suggest a connection to Medieval Latin balietus, from baietus (“page, serving boy”)."},{"parola":"craniu","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"latinismo_dotto","incerta":"no","significato":"skull","termine_origine":"crānium","catena":[{"codice":"la-med","nome":"Medieval Latin","termine":"crānium","categoria":"latinismo_dotto"}],"lontana":"la-med","ambiti":["scienza"],"via":"","testo":"Borrowed from Medieval Latin crānium."},{"parola":"doxologie","pos":"noun","n_etimologia":1,"categoria":"latinismo_dotto","incerta":"no","significato":"doxology","termine_origine":"doxologia","catena":[{"codice":"la-med","nome":"Medieval Latin","termine":"doxologia","categoria":"latinismo_dotto"},{"codice":"grc","nome":"Ancient Greek","termine":"δοξολογία","traslitterazione":"doxología","categoria":"non_precisato"}],"lontana":"grc","ambiti":[],"via":"","testo":"Borrowed from Medieval Latin doxologia, from Ancient Greek δοξολογία (doxología, “a praising”)."},{"parola":"